            "A basket press consists of a cylindrical cage, traditionally wood and now often stainless steel, into which grapes or skins are loaded, with a plate that descends (or in modern vertical versions, a screw or hydraulic ram) to compress the mass while juice escapes through the slats; it is simple, gentle when operated with patience, and still favoured by some quality-focused and traditional producers, though it is labour-intensive to load and unload. Pneumatic (bladder or membrane) presses use a horizontal rotating drum containing an inflatable membrane that expands to press fruit gently and evenly against the perforated drum wall, and are the modern standard because they allow precise, programmable, low-pressure pressing cycles that minimise the extraction of harsh phenolics and reduce oxidation exposure by allowing the drum to be purged with inert gas. Gentler pressing generally yields cleaner juice with less bitterness, harsh tannin or vegetal character, and is favoured for delicate white and sparkling wine production, whereas harder pressing extracts more volume and more phenolic material, useful when a winemaker wants added structure or when maximising yield is a priority. An important timing distinction is that for red wines, pressing normally happens after alcoholic fermentation and maceration are complete, since the winemaker wants extended skin contact with the fermenting must to extract colour and tannin first, whereas for white and rosé wines pressing typically happens before or very early in fermentation, since prolonged skin contact is usually not desired."
