            "Alcoholic fermentation is the biochemical transformation at the heart of winemaking: yeast, principally the species Saccharomyces cerevisiae, metabolises the sugars glucose and fructose in the grape must in the absence of sufficient oxygen (or by preference even when oxygen is present, a behaviour called the Crabtree effect), converting them primarily into ethanol and carbon dioxide gas, along with heat and a wide range of secondary byproducts such as glycerol, higher alcohols, esters and volatile acids that contribute significantly to the wine's aroma and texture. This process continues until the yeast has consumed essentially all the fermentable sugar (producing a dry wine) or until fermentation is deliberately halted or naturally stops, for instance because alcohol levels become toxic to the yeast or because low temperature or nutrient deficiency arrests activity.",
