            "The vessel in which fermentation takes place also shapes the wine. Stainless steel is inert, easy to clean and temperature-control precisely, and preserves primary fruit character without adding any external flavour, making it the standard choice for fresh, fruit-driven styles. Concrete, whether traditional open-top vats or the popular modern 'eggs', is also essentially flavour-neutral but has different thermal properties, moderating temperature swings more slowly than steel, and its shape (particularly the egg form) is thought by some winemakers to encourage gentle internal convection currents that keep lees in suspension. Oak vessels, whether traditional barrels or larger foudres, contribute their own flavour compounds (vanillin, spice, toast-derived aromas) and permit slow oxygen ingress through the wood and around the bung, softening texture even during fermentation. Amphorae and qvevri, fired clay vessels used since antiquity and revived by producers seeking a different textural and flavour outcome, are typically semi-porous, allowing gentle micro-oxygenation, and impart a distinctive earthy, textural quality without the overt vanilla or spice notes of oak. Temperature control during fermentation is critical because yeast activity generates heat and different outcomes are desired for different styles: cooler fermentation temperatures, roughly in the high teens Celsius, slow yeast activity and better preserve delicate, volatile aromatic compounds, making them well suited to aromatic and fresh white wines, whereas warmer fermentation temperatures, into the high twenties or beyond for reds, increase the rate of chemical and enzymatic extraction of tannin, colour pigment (anthocyanins) and flavour compounds from the skins into the must, which is desirable for building structure and depth of colour in red wine but carries the risk, if temperatures run too high, of killing the yeast or driving off delicate aromas."
