            "Maceration is the period during which red must (juice, skins, and often seeds) sits together, allowing colour, tannin, and flavour compounds to diffuse from the solid grape parts into the liquid. Cold soak, or pre-fermentation maceration, holds the crushed must at a low temperature, often refrigerated or achieved with dry ice, for anywhere from a day to a week before alcoholic fermentation begins, deliberately delaying yeast activity. Because ethanol is a more effective solvent for extracting certain compounds than water alone, cold soak favours extraction of water-soluble colour and flavour precursors before alcohol appears, aiming for a fruit-forward, deeply coloured result without pulling excessive tannin, though its actual impact is debated among winemakers and its effectiveness can depend heavily on temperature and grape variety.",
            "During active fermentation, the carbon dioxide produced pushes solid grape material upward to form a compacted cap floating above the liquid, and because this cap is not in full contact with the fermenting must, winemakers use various techniques to keep extraction even and prevent the cap from drying out or harbouring spoilage organisms. Punch-down, or pigeage, physically pushes the cap back down into the liquid using a tool or, in small operations, feet, and is generally considered a gentler technique often used for more delicate varieties. Pump-over, or remontage, draws liquid from the bottom of the vessel and sprays it back over the top of the cap, which both wets and helps break up the cap and introduces some oxygen, useful for yeast health and for softening tannin through a process related to oxidative polymerisation. Rack-and-return, or délestage, drains the entire liquid volume away from the cap into another vessel, leaving the solids briefly exposed, before pumping the liquid back over the top; this more vigorous technique aerates the must strongly and can help settle out harsher, seed-derived tannins along with any unripe or vegetal matter, though it is more labour- and equipment-intensive.",
