            "Oak barrels are produced from split or sawn timber, most commonly from European oak species (particularly Quercus petraea and Quercus robur, often referred to under regional names such as French oak, though also sourced from other European forests) or American oak (Quercus alba), each of which imparts a different flavour signature: European oak tends to be associated with subtler spice, finer-grained tannin and structure, largely due to its tighter grain, while American oak, with a looser grain and different chemical composition, is often linked to more overt coconut, vanilla and sweet spice notes. Other oak sources, such as Hungarian or other Central European oak, are also used and valued for particular character profiles. Staves are seasoned, meaning left outdoors exposed to weather for a period of months to a few years, which leaches out harsh, bitter tannins and allows microbial and enzymatic activity to soften the wood's character before it is shaped into a barrel; the staves are then bent, typically using controlled heat (and sometimes fire) from a brazier, which simultaneously allows the cooper to curve the wood and to apply a toast level to the interior surface, ranging from light to medium to heavy toast, each producing a different balance of aromas such as fresh toast, caramel, coffee, chocolate, clove or smoke.",
