            "Blending is one of the winemaker's most powerful tools, used for several distinct purposes that are often pursued simultaneously. Blending different grape varieties, as in classic Bordeaux-style red blends of Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and other varieties, allows each component to contribute a particular strength, such as structure, colour, aromatic lift or midpalate fruit, producing a wine more balanced and complex than any single variety might achieve alone in that vineyard's conditions. Blending across vineyard parcels or sites lets a winemaker combine fruit from different soils, aspects or micro-climates, each contributing a different nuance, to build a more complete, layered final wine, or alternatively to smooth out weaknesses in any single parcel's fruit in a given year. Blending across vintages is central to the production of non-vintage wines, most notably in Champagne and other traditional-method sparkling wines, where combining a base vintage with reserve wines held back from previous years allows a producer to maintain a consistent house style year after year despite natural vintage variation. Finally, blending oak-aged and unoaked (or differently oaked) lots of the same base wine gives a winemaker fine control over the final balance of fruit purity versus oak-derived spice, toast and structure, allowing a house style to be dialled in with precision rather than being dictated entirely by how each individual lot happened to be aged. Across all these applications, blending trials, carried out by tasting numerous combinations at different proportions, are a core part of the winemaker's craft, balancing consistency, complexity and house or vintage identity."
