            "The ancestral method, sometimes marketed under the term pétillant naturel or pét-nat, is the oldest and simplest approach to sparkling wine, predating the traditional method historically. Rather than undergoing two separate fermentations, the wine undergoes a single fermentation that is intentionally not allowed to finish before bottling. The still-fermenting must is bottled while residual sugar and active yeast remain, and fermentation completes inside the sealed bottle, trapping carbon dioxide as it goes. Classic ancestral method wines are bottled with no dosage and no disgorgement, so the finished wine retains its lees sediment and is often cloudy, though some modern versions do include a partial or full disgorgement step to produce a clearer wine while still retaining the ancestral method's characteristically low intervention and often slightly rustic, sometimes off-dry or funky character.",
