            "Noble rot is a particular and more dramatic form of concentration caused by the fungus Botrytis cinerea under specific climatic conditions, typically a combination of morning humidity or fog that encourages the fungus to take hold, followed by warm, dry afternoons that prevent it from spreading destructively. Under these conditions, Botrytis behaves benevolently rather than as the destructive grey rot it becomes in wetter, cooler conditions: it perforates the grape skin with fine filaments, allowing water to evaporate from the berry while leaving sugar, acid, and flavor compounds behind, dramatically concentrating the grape's must. Botrytis also directly contributes its own distinctive flavor and aroma compounds to affected grapes, commonly described in terms of honey, dried apricot, marmalade, and a particular waxy or saline character, along with glycerol, which adds a viscous, unctuous texture to the finished wine. Because botrytis develops unevenly across a vineyard and even within a single bunch, grapes affected by noble rot are frequently harvested in multiple selective passes through the vineyard, picking only the most botrytis-affected berries or clusters at each pass, which is highly labor-intensive and is one of the reasons why the world's most celebrated noble rot sweet wines, produced in regions with a long-standing reputation for this style, tend to command high prices."
